WEBVTT

00:00.090 --> 00:02.970
-: So if you recall, when I first introduced git commit,

00:02.970 --> 00:06.030
I said that you could use this command, git commit,

00:06.030 --> 00:09.060
on its own, but I recommended against it for the time being

00:09.060 --> 00:11.550
because it will open up the default editor

00:11.550 --> 00:14.970
and that default editor is likely to be Vim,

00:14.970 --> 00:17.100
which I'll just demonstrate very quickly

00:17.100 --> 00:19.860
so you can see what that experience is like.

00:19.860 --> 00:22.110
I'm going to come over to my little,

00:22.110 --> 00:26.040
my first novel project and make a very simple change.

00:26.040 --> 00:28.740
I don't think I have any changes at this moment,

00:28.740 --> 00:31.260
but I see a typo right here in my outline.

00:31.260 --> 00:32.310
So I'm just gonna fix it.

00:32.310 --> 00:36.240
Nick goes home and sees Catsby for the first time.

00:36.240 --> 00:40.080
So I'm gonna save that, git status, okay.

00:40.080 --> 00:43.653
Then I'm gonna do a git add outline.txt.

00:44.520 --> 00:48.600
Then I'm gonna do git commit without the -m.

00:48.600 --> 00:51.150
I'm not gonna pass in a message or any of that.

00:51.150 --> 00:53.880
Now, let's see what happens when I hit enter.

00:53.880 --> 00:55.080
Hmm.

00:55.080 --> 00:56.010
Let me full screen this

00:56.010 --> 00:59.220
so you can get a better picture of what's going on.

00:59.220 --> 01:00.870
This is called Vim.

01:00.870 --> 01:02.190
It's an editor.

01:02.190 --> 01:04.590
It's built into the terminal.

01:04.590 --> 01:07.230
It's very, it can be very confusing

01:07.230 --> 01:09.573
because I can't just start typing.

01:10.470 --> 01:12.750
It's asking me to enter a commit message,

01:12.750 --> 01:14.310
but if I try typing right now,

01:14.310 --> 01:16.350
it's actually not going to type a message.

01:16.350 --> 01:19.740
First, I have to enter what's called the insert mode

01:19.740 --> 01:22.683
by typing I, so if I just hit the letter I.

01:23.520 --> 01:24.720
Now at the bottom left,

01:24.720 --> 01:27.210
can you see that here it says inserts?

01:27.210 --> 01:29.580
By the way, this really is not the time

01:29.580 --> 01:30.660
to be teaching you Vim.

01:30.660 --> 01:31.980
I'm not expecting you to learn it,

01:31.980 --> 01:33.510
but if you end up in this situation,

01:33.510 --> 01:35.400
I want you to know how to get out of it.

01:35.400 --> 01:37.170
But we're gonna change our default editor,

01:37.170 --> 01:39.450
so don't worry about the actual commands.

01:39.450 --> 01:41.940
So I hit I, so that I'm in insert mode,

01:41.940 --> 01:45.120
and now I can start typing and leave a commit message.

01:45.120 --> 01:46.440
So what did I do?

01:46.440 --> 01:47.490
I fixed a typo.

01:47.490 --> 01:51.420
So "Fix Typo," just like that.

01:51.420 --> 01:54.723
Alright? Fix typo in outline maybe.

01:55.890 --> 01:56.723
Sure.

01:58.410 --> 02:00.330
Now, how do I get out of here?

02:00.330 --> 02:01.950
Well, it's also confusing.

02:01.950 --> 02:06.330
First I hit the escape key one time to leave insert mode.

02:06.330 --> 02:09.270
Next, I need to leave Vim entirely.

02:09.270 --> 02:10.860
And to do that, there's a couple of options,

02:10.860 --> 02:15.540
but I'm gonna do colon, W, and then Q.

02:15.540 --> 02:16.470
Oh gosh.

02:16.470 --> 02:17.820
So W stands for write.

02:17.820 --> 02:19.170
Q stands for quit.

02:19.170 --> 02:22.290
So this is write, basically save these changes,

02:22.290 --> 02:25.440
and quit Vim, it's very confusing.

02:25.440 --> 02:27.180
Now I'm gonna hit enter.

02:27.180 --> 02:28.013
Whew.

02:28.013 --> 02:29.280
And we made that commit,

02:29.280 --> 02:31.920
and you can see fix typo in outline.

02:31.920 --> 02:34.620
If I type git log, fix typo in outline.

02:34.620 --> 02:36.090
So it looks exactly the same.

02:36.090 --> 02:37.590
It's a commit.

02:37.590 --> 02:38.670
Nothing different.

02:38.670 --> 02:39.900
It's just the way that we made it

02:39.900 --> 02:43.170
and passed in that message was a lot worse.

02:43.170 --> 02:47.730
Now, there are times where using this message flag,

02:47.730 --> 02:51.240
the -m flag, is not gonna cut it.

02:51.240 --> 02:54.180
If we need to type a much longer commit message,

02:54.180 --> 02:56.250
I mean you could cram it all into a single line,

02:56.250 --> 02:59.190
but on larger projects and large code bases,

02:59.190 --> 03:01.920
open source projects, your commit messages

03:01.920 --> 03:04.620
might actually look something like this.

03:04.620 --> 03:07.890
Multiple lines, maybe paragraphs, bullet points.

03:07.890 --> 03:10.020
So that's not gonna cut it,

03:10.020 --> 03:12.180
just passing it in inside of quotes.

03:12.180 --> 03:13.980
You don't wanna be typing something like that

03:13.980 --> 03:16.710
in your terminal on a single line.

03:16.710 --> 03:19.800
So we do wanna configure this version,

03:19.800 --> 03:22.320
git commit, so that it opens up an editor

03:22.320 --> 03:24.900
where we actually feel comfortable typing paragraphs,

03:24.900 --> 03:26.280
where we could type something like this.

03:26.280 --> 03:27.780
And for the majority of students

03:27.780 --> 03:29.610
and the majority of developers out there,

03:29.610 --> 03:31.650
VIM is not that editor.

03:31.650 --> 03:33.900
So I'd like to use VS Code as my editor.

03:33.900 --> 03:36.630
I've been using it in the course, no surprise there.

03:36.630 --> 03:41.040
But you can configure GI to use any, really any editor.

03:41.040 --> 03:43.080
You can use VS Code or Sublime Text

03:43.080 --> 03:45.660
or any of the other editors that are out there.

03:45.660 --> 03:48.030
I'll show you how to do that right now.

03:48.030 --> 03:50.490
So if you go to the git documentation,

03:50.490 --> 03:54.060
there's this page called git command, set up and config.

03:54.060 --> 03:56.610
It's on the appendix for the book.

03:56.610 --> 03:59.490
I'll just include a link to this if you're curious.

03:59.490 --> 04:02.760
And just like before, when we configured our username

04:02.760 --> 04:06.600
and the email address for our git configuration,

04:06.600 --> 04:08.700
we can do the same thing for the editor.

04:08.700 --> 04:10.560
So depending on what editor you're using,

04:10.560 --> 04:13.920
there's a table here and I'm looking for VS Code,

04:13.920 --> 04:16.860
which is right here, Visual Studio Code.

04:16.860 --> 04:19.440
And this is the command I need to type.

04:19.440 --> 04:21.810
So git config, we'll do global

04:21.810 --> 04:23.760
so that it applies to every repo,

04:23.760 --> 04:25.740
not just the current repo.

04:25.740 --> 04:27.810
Core.editor is what we're configuring.

04:27.810 --> 04:30.450
And I want it to be code, which is the name

04:30.450 --> 04:33.030
of VS Code, Visual Studio Code.

04:33.030 --> 04:36.270
And then I'll talk about this --wait in a moment.

04:36.270 --> 04:38.490
But basically we just copy this line.

04:38.490 --> 04:41.730
So if you're using Sublime, copy this line.

04:41.730 --> 04:44.340
On a Mac or if you're on a PC, copy this line.

04:44.340 --> 04:46.860
If you're using something else, text edit on a Mac,

04:46.860 --> 04:49.890
this is the line, but I'm gonna use VS code.

04:49.890 --> 04:52.050
So now I'll head over to my terminal,

04:52.050 --> 04:53.433
I'll paste this in here.

04:54.450 --> 04:56.580
Okay, we don't see anything change,

04:56.580 --> 04:58.740
but now let's make another commit.

04:58.740 --> 05:01.710
What could we do that won't take too much time?

05:01.710 --> 05:03.120
Okay, here's a simple one.

05:03.120 --> 05:05.550
I have some extra white space in chapter two,

05:05.550 --> 05:06.630
just at the top.

05:06.630 --> 05:09.333
So I'm just gonna get rid of that and save.

05:10.320 --> 05:12.063
And now I'll type git status.

05:13.380 --> 05:15.660
Alright, it says I modified that file.

05:15.660 --> 05:17.880
All I did was delete some empty lines,

05:17.880 --> 05:19.350
but that's still modification.

05:19.350 --> 05:23.820
So I'm gonna add that, git add chapter2.txt.

05:23.820 --> 05:27.480
Now, I would usually just use git commit -m

05:27.480 --> 05:29.610
because it's really just a simple message.

05:29.610 --> 05:30.900
We did something so simple,

05:30.900 --> 05:33.540
there's no point in typing a paragraph, right?

05:33.540 --> 05:35.970
You wanna keep your commit messages as short as possible,

05:35.970 --> 05:38.280
but there are times where you need more information.

05:38.280 --> 05:41.760
This is not really one, but I'm gonna do just git commit

05:41.760 --> 05:43.770
without the -m flag just to demonstrate

05:43.770 --> 05:47.820
that hopefully our editor is configured to be VS code.

05:47.820 --> 05:49.560
Hmm. Let's see.

05:49.560 --> 05:53.850
There's a problem with the editor code --wait.

05:53.850 --> 05:56.130
So this is a problem stemming from the fact

05:56.130 --> 05:59.370
that my terminal does not know what code

05:59.370 --> 06:01.440
the command code is referring to.

06:01.440 --> 06:03.960
So it's telling me here, command not found.

06:03.960 --> 06:07.020
Basically, if I go into VS code, I can fix that.

06:07.020 --> 06:11.193
I'm gonna open the command pallet, which is command shift P,

06:12.030 --> 06:15.090
and then I'm gonna type code,

06:15.090 --> 06:18.813
and I want this, install code command in path.

06:20.370 --> 06:21.570
Okay?

06:21.570 --> 06:23.910
So it says shell command code

06:23.910 --> 06:25.650
successfully installed in path.

06:25.650 --> 06:27.600
So now hopefully this works.

06:27.600 --> 06:30.690
It knows what the command "code" is referring to.

06:30.690 --> 06:32.460
So let's try committing again.

06:32.460 --> 06:36.300
Aha. Now it's opened up in VS Code.

06:36.300 --> 06:37.350
And you can see over here,

06:37.350 --> 06:39.990
it's waiting for me to close this file.

06:39.990 --> 06:44.850
So that's why we had to add that --wait right here.

06:44.850 --> 06:47.040
We wanted to wait until we finish editing

06:47.040 --> 06:48.990
and save our commit message.

06:48.990 --> 06:50.700
So I can type my commit in here.

06:50.700 --> 06:52.380
Also, I didn't mention this earlier.

06:52.380 --> 06:53.850
Everything that starts with a hash

06:53.850 --> 06:56.520
or the octothorp character will be ignored.

06:56.520 --> 06:57.353
It's a comment.

06:57.353 --> 06:58.773
It's not included in the commit.

06:59.640 --> 07:01.500
So, in here, what did we just do?

07:01.500 --> 07:02.333
I've already forgotten.

07:02.333 --> 07:04.440
Oh, we removed white space from chapter two.

07:04.440 --> 07:06.300
So you know,

07:06.300 --> 07:09.840
remove extra white space

07:09.840 --> 07:13.383
or extra lines in chapter two or something.

07:14.640 --> 07:15.570
Well, that kind of sounds like

07:15.570 --> 07:18.420
we actually deleted lines of chapter two.

07:18.420 --> 07:20.343
How about extra blank lines?

07:21.270 --> 07:22.860
Sure. Okay.

07:22.860 --> 07:25.530
So now I'm gonna save this file,

07:25.530 --> 07:26.940
if that's the commit message I want,

07:26.940 --> 07:28.980
but I could put a lot more in here

07:28.980 --> 07:31.080
and maybe just to demonstrate that I will,

07:31.950 --> 07:36.360
why don't I just copy one of these example commit messages

07:36.360 --> 07:37.320
that I was talking about?

07:37.320 --> 07:39.960
I'm just gonna copy this entire thing over

07:39.960 --> 07:42.930
and this is just to show you that we can do this.

07:42.930 --> 07:46.620
Alright, so here's my much longer commit message.

07:46.620 --> 07:50.370
All these lines, paragraphs of text, some bullet points.

07:50.370 --> 07:53.040
They won't be formatted in any particular way.

07:53.040 --> 07:54.810
I have to do this manually, right?

07:54.810 --> 07:57.540
If I want that bullet point, it's just a dash.

07:57.540 --> 08:00.360
But anyway, this is my commit message here.

08:00.360 --> 08:02.760
Really just the first line that matters.

08:02.760 --> 08:05.733
I'm gonna save, I'm gonna close the file,

08:07.020 --> 08:10.860
and then over here, it now made the commit.

08:10.860 --> 08:13.500
It was waiting and now it's not, it finished.

08:13.500 --> 08:16.410
So if I type git log, what do we see?

08:16.410 --> 08:18.900
Ooh, well, we made that commit.

08:18.900 --> 08:21.870
It is here, but it's taking up so much space.

08:21.870 --> 08:24.060
That's really, really annoying.

08:24.060 --> 08:24.990
So in the next video,

08:24.990 --> 08:28.530
I'll show you how we can use git log with a small tweak

08:28.530 --> 08:30.750
so that we don't have to see the entire commit message

08:30.750 --> 08:33.180
in the case of a very long commit message.

08:33.180 --> 08:35.430
Anyway, we set up the editor,

08:35.430 --> 08:38.070
we configured it to use VS Code in this video,

08:38.070 --> 08:40.470
if you're doing something else like Sublime Text,

08:40.470 --> 08:42.060
follow the instructions here.

08:42.060 --> 08:44.490
I'll include this link in the description

08:44.490 --> 08:45.720
if you wanna check it out.

08:45.720 --> 08:47.700
But you can avoid this for the most part

08:47.700 --> 08:50.580
by just using -m until you really need to type

08:50.580 --> 08:52.020
a longer commit message,

08:52.020 --> 08:54.780
which is not all that common when you're starting out.

08:54.780 --> 08:56.820
Once you start working on larger projects though,

08:56.820 --> 08:58.140
it's definitely important to be able

08:58.140 --> 09:00.623
to open up and write messages in an editor.

09:00.623 --> 09:01.503
Okay.
